{"sounds":{"intro":"This is Attic Greek of the 5th and 4th centuries BC, the language of Plato, Sophocles and Thucydides. It is written in the Greek alphabet. The Latin line is a simple transliteration and the third line gives a reconstructed classical pronunciation. Nobody speaks it, so the pronunciation is a scholars' reconstruction.","rows":[["a","ah, like \"father\"","ἀγορά (agora)"],["e / ē","\"e\" in \"bed\" / a long \"ay\"","ἐγώ / ἦν"],["o / ō","\"o\" in \"more\" / a long \"aw\"","ἄνθρωπος / ὦ"],["u","like French \"u\" or German \"ü\"","ὕδωρ (hydor)"],["ph / th / kh","p, t, k with a puff of air","φίλος, θεός, χορός"],["ei","a long \"ay\", like \"bay\"","εἶναι"],["ou","like \"oo\" in \"food\"","οὐ"]],"notes":["Ancient Greek has marks above the vowels: breathings (᾿ and ῾) and accents (´ ` ῀). A rough breathing (῾) means an \"h\" sound at the start of a word: ὁ (ho).","This pool is much smaller than the Spanish one on purpose. Only well-attested words and forms are included. Nothing has been checked by a classicist, so treat the accents as a first guide."]},"numbers":[{"id":"1-to-10","title":"1 to 10","items":[["one","heis","HAYS","εἷς"],["two","dyo","DEW-oh","δύο"],["three","treis","TRAYS","τρεῖς"],["four","tettares","TET-tah-res","τέτταρες"],["five","pente","PEN-teh","πέντε"],["six","hex","HEKS","ἕξ"],["seven","hepta","hep-TAH","ἑπτά"],["eight","okto","ok-TAW","ὀκτώ"],["nine","ennea","EN-neh-ah","ἐννέα"],["ten","deka","DEH-kah","δέκα"]]},{"id":"bigger-numbers","title":"Bigger numbers","items":[["twenty","eikosi","AY-ko-see","εἴκοσι"],["one hundred","hekaton","heh-KAH-ton","ἑκατόν"],["one thousand","khilioi","KHEE-lee-oy","χίλιοι"]]}],"greetings":[{"id":"hello-and-goodbye","title":"Hello and goodbye","items":[["hello / goodbye","khaire","KHAY-reh","χαῖρε"],["hello / goodbye (to many)","khairete","KHAY-reh-teh","χαίρετε"],["farewell","errhoso","er-RHAW-saw","ἔρρωσο"],["how are you?","ti prattei","tee PRAT-tay","τί πράττεις;"],["well","eu","EH-oo","εὖ"],["very w